Steps to install:

  1. $ git clone --recursive git@github.com:jyrikimari/mlkmc.git
  2. On desktop:
     $ make

     should be sufficient. The QUIP library will be built as
     a part of compilation, and you will be prompted to make
     choices in QUIP's make config. The default option should
     work for everything EXCEPT
     "Would you like to compile with GAP support ? [y/n]"
     Here you should choose y instead of the default n.

     On clusters, you will need to load the appropriate modules
     and build the appropriate target. Support currently extends
     to the CSC Puhti cluster, the NSC Tetralith cluster,
     and the Helsinki University Turso cluster federation.

     When QUIP's make config prompts you to choose
     "Would you like to compile with preconditioned minimisation support? (not working with Intel compiler) [y/n]",
     it is most likely wise to choose n if you are using Intel
     compiler, as instructed here for Puhti and Tetralith.

     On Puhti:
     $ module load intel-oneapi-compilers intel-oneapi-mpi intel-oneapi-mkl
     $ make puhti

     On Tetralith:
     $ module load buildenv-intel/2023a-eb
     $ make tetralith

     On Turso:
     $ module load OpenMPI OpenBLAS LAPACK
     $ make turso

   The way LAMMPS is linked requires setting
   $ export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/path/to/mlkmc/lammps/src:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH
   before running, both on desktop and on clusters.

Running on desktop (mainly for testing/developing):

  You need an in.mlkmc file in the working directory (examples
  provided), and a LAMMPS style interatomic potential energy
  function in the place indicated in in.mlkmc. Then, if
  the mlkmc directory have been added to your PATH, to run:
  $ mpirun -n 11 --oversubscribe mlkmc

  The number of MPI tasks, here 11, is equal to the default number
  of NEB replicas in the barrier calculations. If you change
  the parameter neb_images in in.mlkmc, change the number of
  mpirun -n parameter accordingly. On a desktop, you most likely
  need the --oversubscribe flag to have enough "cores" available.

  On a desktop, you may want to direct stdout to a file, by e.g.
  $ mpirun -n 11 --oversubscribe mlkmc | tee mlkmc.out

Running on clusters:

  In the sbatch script, set -N 1, --ntasks-per-node=11 and --cpus-per-task=1.
  Then (again if mlkmc can be found in your PATH), include the following commands
  in the script, depending on the platform.

  On Puhti:
  module load intel-oneapi-compilers intel-oneapi-mpi intel-oneapi-mkl
  ulimit -Ss unlimited
  srun mlkmc

  On Tetralith:
  module load buildenv-intel/2023a-eb
  ulimit -Ss unlimited
  srun mlkmc

  On Turso:
  module load OpenMPI OpenBLAS LAPACK
  srun --mpi=pmix mlkmc

Running the examples:

  Examples are included for simulating diffusion and deposition
  of Ag and CuAgAu on the Ag {111} substrate in examples/Ag_111/,
  and deposition of W on W {110} substrate in examples/W_on_W_110/.
  Each simulation is limited to 10 minutes of wall time in
  the input file. In each run, the program will either
  generate the initial substrate system as instructed in in.mlkmc,
  or read an initial.system file if present. The output is a
  log in stdout, an out.xyz file of the simulation, and a
  predictors/ directory with the collection of barrier data
  points and ML models as they existed at the end of the
  simulation. Additionally, intermediate and final restart
  files are written.

  Ag_111:

    These simulations require the CuAgAu.eam.fs potential file
    which can be downloaded from
    https://www.ctcms.nist.gov/potentials/entry/1990--Ackland-G-J-Vitek-V--Cu-Ag-Au/.
    The simulations are set up to read the same copy of the
    file from examples/Ag_111/ (i.e., there's no need to make
    a separate copy in each subdirectory).

    Ag_diffusion:
      Place one random Ag atom on Ag {111} at 300 K and track its
      mean square displacement throughout the simulation.
      Will only execute one NEB calculation, so the simulation
      runs quite fast. The  predictors/ directory will have a
      file named hop_predictor_3_3_1.xml that holds the result of the
      NEB run for terrace diffusion.

    Ag_deposition:
      You may copy the predictors/ directory and initial.system
      from the previous simulation to speed up the startup.
      The simulation will deposit Ag atoms on Ag {111} at 300 K
      at a rate of 10^5 monolayers/s.

    CuAgAu_deposition:
      Since the previous simulations were initialised to
      only include Ag atoms, the initial.system and the predictors/
      directory are not reusable here. This simulation will
      concurrently deposit Cu atoms at a rate of 3*10^4 monolayers/s,
      Ag at 4*10^3 monolayers/s, and Au at 3*10^4 monolayers/s
      at 300 K.

  W_on_W_110:

      This simulation will read the W_zhou.eam.alloy in
      lammps/potentials, which is part of the LAMMPS submodule
      installation. No need to make a copy.

      This simulation will deposit W atoms on the W {110} surface
      at 300 K at a rate of 10^(-5) monolayers/s.

